.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Rabin&#x27;s 1980 randomization of Miller&#x27;s test declares n &quot;probably prime&quot; after k random witnesses; for a composite n each witness is fooled with probability at most 1/4, so the error shrinks like 4^{-k}. This script shows the test exposing a Carmichael number that fools Fermat&#x27;s test, measures the error rate against the number of rounds for two hard composites, and certifies a 39-digit prime far beyond trial division&#x27;s reach.">

.. only:: html

  .. image:: /api/gallery/number_theory/primality/images/thumb/sphx_glr_plot_01_miller_rabin_thumb.png
    :alt:

  :doc:`/api/gallery/number_theory/primality/plot_01_miller_rabin`

.. raw:: html

      <div class="sphx-glr-thumbnail-title">The Miller-Rabin probabilistic primality test</div>
    </div>


.. only:: not html

 * :doc:`/api/gallery/number_theory/primality/plot_01_miller_rabin`

.. raw:: html

    <div class="sphx-glr-thumbcontainer" tooltip="Euler&#x27;s \varphi(n) counts the integers in 1, \dots, n coprime to n. His 1763 theorem, a^{\varphi(n)} \equiv 1 \pmod n for \gcd(a, n) = 1, generalizes Fermat&#x27;s little theorem (\varphi(p) = p - 1 for a prime p). This script checks the definition and the theorem and plots \varphi(n), whose top edge n - 1 is traced by the primes.">

